 not align with the principle because if one does not criticize a form of behavior in oneself, they should not criticize it in others.Therefore, this option does not justify the application of the principle.\n\n3.**Option C**: McFeney often criticizes Shimada for being tardy, but neither Shimada nor McFeney ever vows to cease being tardy.\nThis option implies that Shimada is criticizing McFeney for tardiness while also criticizing himself. This situation aligns with the principle because if one criticizes a form of behavior in oneself, they can also criticize it in others. Therefore, this option justifies the application of the principle.. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
