ent where the outcome (Popov winning) is dependent on the condition (election being unfair).\n\n4.**Option B Analysis:**.\n   - Kapinski, our new neighbor, is probably friendly.\n   - Kapinski sells insurance, and most people who sell insurance are friendly.\n   - This option involves a generalization based on a specific characteristic (selling insurance).\n\n5.**Option C Analysis:**.\n   - If Clarissa missed the bus today, it is quite unlikely that she would have gotten to work on time.\n   - So, it is quite unlikely that Clarissa missed the bus, since she actually was at work on time today.This option involves a conditional statement where the outcome (Clarissa missing the bus) is independent of the condition (Clarissa being at work on time).. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
