ns the rarity of two-truck collisions, which doesn't necessarily imply that triple-trailer trucks would be more effective.\n     - **Conclusion:** This option does not weaken the argument significantly.\n\n   - **B) No matter what changes Partoria makes in the regulation of trucking, it will have to keep some smaller roads off-limits to all large trucks.**.\n     - This statement suggests that there are limitations to how much Partoria can regulate trucking.It implies that even if they increase the use of triple-trailer trucks, they might still face restrictions on certain roads.\n     - **Conclusion:** This option weakens the argument by suggesting that the effectiveness of using triple-trailer trucks might be limited due to regulatory constraints.\nThe answer is D. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
